Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for its decision to quarantine Spain.
Dominic Raab defended the government’s sudden decision to reintroduce quarantine measures in Spain.
The ministers announced on Saturday that holidaymakers returning to Spain and its islands after midnight would be required to self-isolate for 14 days if they failed to do so.
Spain has been on the list of “travel corridors” that can safely travel as set out at Number 10 Downing Street, but recent increases in coronavirus cases have raised public concerns about a second wave of the pandemic.
On Sunday, the Foreign Secretary made the case that this measure was a "real-time response" to the sudden reimposition of travel restrictions following a surge in reported infections in the region.
He stated that he had assessed the situation the previous afternoon, and "we made a quick decision."
He added, “We do not apologize for this.”
“We must be able to take swift and decisive action, especially in matters pertaining to our homeland or Spain, and in particular countries.”
Otherwise, Britain would face another infection risk, triggering a second wave of the pandemic, and then have to implement another lockdown.
The Minister of Transport faces mandatory quarantine like holidaymakers going to Spain.
Following two consecutive days of reporting more than 900 new coronavirus cases per day in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists were not able to cancel or alter their travel plans in time.
He said, “I understand that for those in Spain or considering going to Spain, this decision is very harsh, but we must have the ability to take swift and decisive action to protect the UK.”
"We have made notable progress in reducing the case numbers and preventing a resurgence of the epidemic."
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth condemned the government's handling as “chaotic.”
He said the sudden decision at Downing Street left holidaymakers "confused and disappointed."
This Labour Party member told Sophie Ridge, "I understand why they made this decision, but their decision-making over the past 24 hours has been chaotic."
Holidaymakers in Spain right now are feeling confused and frustrated, and so are those planning to go on holiday to Spain and the Canary Islands, who don’t know whether their employers will accept them being quarantined for two weeks.
"The government is just saying 'we hope employers will cooperate.'"
"They made this decision without any discernible pattern in the past 24 hours."
The CDC notes that Salmonella is a bacterial foodborne illness that causes 1.35 million infections annually, leads to hospitalization of 26,500 people, and results in 420 deaths.
Common symptoms of salmonella infection include diarrhea, fever, and stomach cramps, which may start showing within six hours to six days of exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last for four to seven days, and most people recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
California couple attacked at dog park, both forgoing masks during lunchtime (video) – RT News USA
First there was mask羞辱, then there were beatings.
A couple in California encountered this when having lunch with their dog at a dog park – a woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they were not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley say they were lunching at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Bassett Hound on Thursday.
As the couple took off their masks to eat, an "old lady who had suddenly appeared" came over and called them "idiots" because they were not wearing masks, even though they said "how can we eat with masks on."
O’Brien forced the woman out of the park and stopped her harassment at which point she sprayed pepper spray at them.
The video taken by bystanders begins with Kelley fighting for the spray can with a woman, and he ends up spraying himself in the face.
After the attackers left with their dogs, O'Brien can be heard crying in the background.
O'Brien uploaded the video to Facebook and told ABC 10 that he hopes the woman "goes to jail."
California mandates masking everywhere, even in fresh air areas.
People can only remove their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
However, the state’s COVID-19 guidelines do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to enforce this requirement.
Even in this “mask shaming” era, it is a crime that will result in a $1,000 fine or up to three years in state prison.
The urchin from Indore who was overturned for refusing a bribe to a cabdriver later received a free apartment and 100 rupees in gratification.
According to reports, on Thursday, Paras Raykar's car overturned after it was filled with eggs by local municipal officials in Indore.
The viral story of a 13-year-old boy who failed to sell his eggs and whose car was overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy from Indore received support from across the country to help them recover from their losses and restart their business.
The boy's family also said that politicians including Nationalist Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had reached out to them and offered help as well.
The boy's family also received an apartment as a gift and a promise to cover their children's educational expenses.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar's cart selling eggs was overturned by Indore municipal officials, as the city implemented a staggered opening from left to right amid the pandemic.
In a video, a boy says that officials warned him earlier that day that they would flip his car if he did not pay a bribe of 100 rupees. The video quickly went viral on social media.
The boy says that when he refused to hand over the 100 rupees, those officials overturned his car and smashed all the eggs.
The boy also says in the video that due to the impact of the pandemic, daily sales of eggs have declined, and these losses are an additional blow to him.
Since Thursday, the family of the boy has received support from all over the country, with many people offering cash or kind help.
The locals also extended help to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh funded them and helped the children enroll in a college; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also assured that a flat would be provided to the boy's family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Press Club also provided food and money for the boy.
The office of Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also offered assistance.
Paras' grandfather said, "We have received support from many people across the country."
(RMT Member) Ramesh Mendola assures us that he will provide us with an apartment according to the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and a资助 of ₹2,500.
Digvijaya Singh pledged to fund Rs 10,000 and promised to look after the education of the two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to offer help.
A female staffer of Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal in Delhi also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also offered support.”
The Home Secretary Priti Patel has asked Twitter and Instagram to explain inappropriate content posted by Wiley.
Due to Twitter and Instagram’s failure to promptly remove inappropriate content posted by Wiley, Priti Patel requested explanations from both platforms.
Police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic comments posted by this Grime artist on social media.
The Minister of Home Affairs tweeted on Sunday: "Wiley's anti-Semitic content is deeply abhorrent."
Twitter and Instagram have not promptly deleted these posts, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.”
He criticized Twitter for "ignoring antisemitism" because Wiley's tweets remained visible 12 hours after they were posted.
Subsequently, the website deleted multiple tweets and banned the account of the 41-year-old singer for seven days.
Twitter previously stated that it had temporarily suspended Wiley's account "for violating our policy on hate speech." Facebook said that "hate speech is not allowed on Instagram."
On Saturday, the NYPD said, "The NYPD is extremely concerned about all reports of antisemitism. We are currently evaluating the relevant materials."
Meanwhile, Wiley's A-List agency has also severed all ties with the celebrity.
The ex-agent of the singer, John Woolf, who is Jewish, wrote on Twitter: “Given the anti-Semitic content Wiley posted today, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all ties with him. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society.”
Earlier, he had stated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley’s comments but would instead engage with him privately and "educate" him.
However, an Instagram video appears to show Wiley cutting ties with his management company.
Other lawmakers also criticized Twitter for failing to remove the tweets promptly.
Labour MP Jess Phillips said on Saturday: "I just saw what Wiley published. Why is @Twitter allowing such blatant antisemitism and hate content to remain? It involves dangerous views: Jews have things you don't have, they control everything, they think they're superior, and so on. Such content is dangerous and should be deleted."
Rep. Neil Coyle added, “His management appears to be faster than @Twitter in responding. I repeat: laws (including the Online Harms Bill) should ensure that social media platforms are not used to spread hate.”
Government anti-Semitic independent adviser John Mann called the tweets "a precise reflection of why legal changes are needed to hold people accountable."
The campaign against antisemitism also called on police to investigate Wiley’s content and to call for the closure of his social media accounts “to prevent another outbreak of anti-Semitic venom.”
Wiley, whose real name is Richard Cowie, is known as the "Grime Music Father" and was awarded an MBE in 2018 for his contributions to music.
Kudlow says a next coronavirus relief package will include more stimulus checks and extend eviction moratoriums.
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow promised on Sunday that the federal government would extend the moratorium on evictions and distribute another round of stimulus checks according to the next coronavirus relief plan.
Before Treasury Secretary Kudlow made his commitment, the White House’s plan for payroll tax cuts had been vetoed by Congress.
Residents of Massart Mallawi village declined to comment on the incident and expressed condolences to the family of the children only.
Mr. Khalid Darwish, advisor to the National Council for Childhood and Motherhood, said in October that approximately 93% of Egyptian children between the ages of 1 and 14 have suffered from violence.
He also noted that prosecution is one of the most important means to eliminate child abuse, and stressed the need for enhanced cooperation between the Committee and the public prosecutors in this regard.
A study by the National Commission on Women and Children found that children could also suffer violence in places that are supposed to be quite safe, such as their homes, schools, or clubs, where perpetrators could include those who are supposed to care for them – such as parents or teachers.
Reuters criticized White House Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany for her "political" briefing, but reporters keep asking political questions – RT TV on American news.
Reuters criticized Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany of the White House for politicizing press briefings more than making them informative, worrying the media, but apparently forgetting that it is the journalists who chose those questions.
The largest US news agency, the Associated Press, published an article on Saturday stating that White House press briefings "may have broken tradition," as McEnany turned the briefings into "brief and high-profile campaigning and attacking the media" for seeking a presidential re-election, while there was "relatively scarce information" about what the government had done.
The last review is understandable, as very little new information was disclosed at the launch event.
What the Associated Press does not mention is that virtually no one asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use White House press officers to draw attention to themselves, and Jim Acosta of CNN has perfected this tactic.
They transcend the typical adversarial relationship between the media and government, repeatedly asking loaded questions to doubt President Donald Trump.
In some cases, they choose to become the news themselves, such as when CBS reporter Weijia Jiang made an unambiguous implication in response to President Trump’s retort of “ask China”: “Mr. Xian, why did you address this to me specifically?”
The launch on July 6 is a good example of how it is no longer a source of reliable information for American citizens.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR ratings had declined because of false reporting about so-called hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and because he decided to ban displaying the Confederate flag during events.
Just as expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions related to this tweet, trying to get her to publicly admit that Trump supports the Confederate flag.
McEnany did not allow the reporters to get away with it, as saying so would damage Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the confederate flag, and his tweets are about NASCAR fans being wrongly portrayed as racists.
The Associated Press reports that she sounded "flushed with anger" during the press conference.
AP's story echoes with an earlier column by ABC News reporter Jonathan Karl, who is also the chairman of the White House Correspondents' Association, published in The Washington Post.
Karl says, "denying reality and using the stage of the White House for purely political purposes goes against public trust."
McEnany assumed the position of press secretary in April and hosted her first briefing on May 1st, displaying her quick wit and combative nature, which made some Trump supporters view her as a hero.
Before she was appointed, the position of press officer had been vacant for more than a year.
Like other aspects of Washington's political life, the relationship between the White House press secretary during Trump's time and the media underwent a significant change.
This change came from both sides, having already occurred prior to McEnany's arrival.
At the White House Correspondents’ Dinner in April 2018, comedian Michelle Wolf subjected former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders to vicious questioning, even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it "shocking."
AP’s White House reporter Jill Colvin asked McEnany at her first press conference, “Can you promise never to lie to us from that podium?”
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume, a veteran of more than five decades in journalism and formerly the ABC's chief White House correspondent from 1989 to 1996, says today’s reporters show "aggressive behavior" during press conferences because they look down on Trump.
He said on Fox News's Tucker Carlson Tonight this week that it's because journalists have focused on countering Trump and have neglected gathering policy news.
"This is not just a game of cat and mouse," Hume said in discussing the media's intended purpose.
Our goal is not just to have government officials lose face, but to obtain information.
California protesters set fire to a courthouse
Oakland (California) – Oakland police said that late Saturday night, peaceful protests in California turned into violent incidents. Protesters set fire to a courthouse, smashed police stations, and attacked several officers.
The Auckland authorities reported on Twitter that demonstrators smashed windows, daubed graffiti, and fired fireworks and lasers at police officers.
Some posted on Twitter calling for peace, with tweets requesting organizers to "help us provide safe spaces and locations for demonstrators."
Protests started on Saturday evening, with groups involved including “Mom’s Wall,” similar to an organization in Portland, Oregon. At the same time, protesters confronted police officers deployed to secure federal courts where they were working.
Since the May 25 incident in Minneapolis where George Floyd was killed, President Donald Trump has sent federal police to suppress protests every night.
Initially, the protesters were holding peaceful demonstrations in Auckland, but they turned violent in the evening.
Around 11:30 PM, the police declared the demonstration an "illegal assembly" and ordered the protesters to disperse.
According to news reports, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short time.
Police said the protesters initially "cracked windows and made racist slurs against residents."
Photos posted by relevant departments show broken glass on the windows of the police station and graffiti on the walls.
UN senior officials canceled their visit to Pakistan due to flight reasons.
Islamabad (AP) – The incoming president of the United Nations General Assembly announced on Sunday that his visit to Pakistan had been delayed “due to technical problems with the flights.”
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir was successfully elected as the President of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, originally scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that his visit to the Pakistani capital had been postponed.
Pakistan's Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweeted in response to Bozkir, saying "We look forward to your swift visit to Pakistan for a constructive and productive visit."
Just one day before, Qureshi had excitedly announced his visit, hoping to discuss issues such as Kashmir with Bozkir, which is located between Pakistan and its neighboring country India, and both claim sovereignty over the entire region of Kashmir.
Since achieving independence from British rule in 1947, there have been three wars between Pakistan and India, both of which involved the disputed region.
Frequent clashes between the two sides in Kashmir have resulted in casualties among troops and civilians.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people living in the Himalayan region to decide whether to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for a solution to the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United secured a spot in the Champions League, and Paul Pogba hinted he would remain with the team.
Paul Pogba, Manchester United star, hinted that he would continue to stay with the club this summer after the Red Devils secured a place in the quarterfinals.
Snatching Leicester City means Manchester United will finish third, ahead of Chelsea under Frank Lampard.
Before 12 more games, Leicester City was still leading Manchester United by 14 points, but the Red Devils made a strong run to reach the quarter-finals.
Pogba has twice requested to leave the team over the past two years, thus fans expect him to depart this summer.
However, the pandemic means that few teams will be spending heavily this summer, and there is no buyer who can afford both the transfer fee and the wages needed to lure Pogba away from Old Trafford.
Making such a statement has been helpful for Pogba in recent weeks, as both his performance and body language have improved.
After winning the match 2-0, the French player posted on Instagram suggesting that he would stay with the team.
While Pogba may simply be celebrating Juventus' return to Champions League qualification, many fans interpret it as a strong hint that he intends to remain at the club to compete with top European players.
Pogba's current contract has one year remaining, but the club can extend it for another year, meaning he would effectively be with the team for two more years.
Manchester United would like to extend Paul Pogba’s contract and hope to clarify his situation before next season.
For Pogba, he feels comfortable in the team, and Bruno Fernandes' arrival has given him an additional boost.
The Portuguese midfielder became the heart of Old Trafford, allowing Pogba to advance deeper into the midfield and also improving his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Wolverhampton Wanderers to Premier League relegation
Some of Watford's players knelt to the ground while others walked away without looking back from the field.
The whistle for沃特福德's return to Premier League after a five-year absence had barely sounded, but nobody could deny that without a fight,沃特福德 would have fallen short when it needed to chase Arsenal without losing a match.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and relentless performance in five years, coming back from a three-point deficit but ultimately losing to Arsenal.
Just 16 minutes before the end of the match, Danny Welbeck shot with a heel strike, and goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez saved the ball. Had he scored, Welbeck would have earned three points for Watford, equaling Arsenal's score and securing their spot in the Premier League.
But in the end, Watford failed to achieve this, and their caretaker manager Hayden Mullins stated that if their previous form had been as good as it was in this match, he would not have had to exert himself so much.
Throughout the season, Watford has been lagging behind, and owner Pozzo's management of the club has been chaotic.
A failed campaign may not destroy an empire, but the Pozzo family cannot lay the foundations for Watford to secure its return to the Premier League.
The frequent changes in managers at Watford have become an inescapable fate.
On rough calculation, the next reshuffle will be the 12th in eight years, but the influx of fresh blood from a change of leadership is limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with the team despite still being eligible for the UAE Super League.
Some players may still depart, such as attacking midfielder Ismaïla Sarr, who again expressed that playing in League Two in September is unbelievable, but preserving their strength remains the top priority at the moment.
Arsenal manager Mikel Arteta stated that the unpredictability of the match outcome between the two teams makes the game more exciting.
Arteta also said that Watford's forwards have been "cheating," before switching to the footballing term "struggling to stay up."
Saying so, Arsenal is not without justification; Arsenal leads the match, and Watford must close the gap by playing aggressively to catch up.
Watford conceded a penalty kick right at the start.
In the first 40 seconds, Craig Dawson accidentally tripped Alexandre Lacazette, and after a lengthy verification of about three minutes via VAR, a penalty kick was awarded. Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang scored the penalty kick.
Watford pressed hard, and Sarr quickly counter-attacked with the ball on the right side, with Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover as they closed in on the goal.
A former Labour Party member told The Daily Mail that even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party, they would still bring legal action.
What I mean is, he really brought the Labor Party back into competitiveness.
He still has a long way to go in terms of his policies, but he has already made a very impressive start. He has succeeded in convincing many who had previously dropped out of the Labour Party and were no longer Labour voters to rejoin the party and regard him as a serious person to take seriously."
Blair indeed fears that the Labour Party is still seen as a party that only raises taxes.
He says, “Based on my experience, if you’re a member of the Labour Party, you need to be very careful with taxation issues, because the public is not sitting there worrying whether you want to raise taxes; they are more likely to worry that you might raise them, so you need to watch out for that mindset.”
COVID-19 will permanently alter the world economy (Opinion)
Just as various security measures became ubiquitous after the 9/11 events, virus screenings may become a part of our lives as well.
Necessary infrastructure investments to meet future viral testing needs are imperative.
If coronavirus immunity provides only short-term protection, this investment can provide economic safeguards.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented similar wage subsidy policies akin to Germany's Kurzarbeit.
According to this policy, both the working hours and wages of workers will be reduced, with the shortfall in wages subsidized by the government.
This move helps to maintain the bond between enterprises and workers, laying a solid foundation for a rapid economic recovery.
Improving the implementation of these policies is also important, which need to become a permanent part of economic recovery tools.
Remote work may become increasingly common.
There is evidence that working from home can be just as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to accept the remote working arrangement.
Given that remote work has been widely tried and proved effective, this approach may continue.
With e-commerce continuing to grow stronger, telemedicine, video conferencing, online teaching, and fintech are being increasingly adopted. The pandemic crisis has also accelerated the pace of digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains are dealing with various shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many such companies shifting production back to their home countries.
Finance Minister Fiona Hyslop has said that the pandemic has had a "catastrophic impact" on all activities.
The restrictions put in place to prevent the spread of the coronavirus have affected Scottish festive activities and sporting events like Highland Games.
Mrs. Hyslop said, “While we have taken an active step, such as the resumption of drive-in movie theaters, many sectors will still be unable to operate for some time.”
We have collaborated closely and constructively with industry stakeholders, who have proposed creative solutions for how to operate safely in light of their respective challenges.
In addition to direct funding, some of the funds will be used to promote innovation and sustain long-term recovery of the event activities.”
The Event Industry Advisory Group notes that the sector was among the first to be restricted by lockdowns and will likely also be one of the last to see comprehensive easing of restrictions.
Chairman of the group, Peter Duthie, who also serves as the CEO of the Glasgow Trading Commission, states that such sporting events bring nearly 6 billion pounds to Scotland annually.
He said, “It is necessary for the government to provide additional funding to the industry, which has been widely supported by event organizers and numerous supply chain companies that have suffered severe economic losses during long lockdowns.”
Shivraj Singh Chouhan Confirmed to Have COVID-19
Himself having tested positive for COVID-19, Chief Minister Shivraj Singh Chouhan of Madhya Pradesh confirmed Saturday. In his tweet published this morning, he said he was “okay” while praising those working on the frontline in the fight against the pandemic.
The top minister reiterated his warning from yesterday, urging everyone to follow防疫 guidelines, such as maintaining social distance and wearing masks.
"Friends, I'm fine. The frontline warriors risk their lives selflessly and deserve praise. I salute all the frontline warriors nationwide," Chowhan wrote on Twitter from Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, where he was admitted yesterday.
Maintain a distance of two meters, wash your hands frequently, and wear masks – these are the most effective weapons we have against the coronavirus,” he said, “I hope everyone can use these weapons to protect themselves and their families.”
Zhouhan added finally, “Be careful everyone, I hope you all stay safe and healthy.” This is also what I pray to God for.
On Saturday, Chouhan tweeted that his viral test result was positive, despite his best efforts to avoid it, and warned, "A slightest lapse of carelessness might get you the coronavirus."
This morning, Chouhan said on Twitter that although the threat of infection remained, there was "no need to be afraid," and called for those with symptoms to get tested.
He says, "If you get infected with the virus, there's no need to be afraid. Do not hide your symptoms; inform the doctor immediately and begin treatment. Early treatment can lead to recovery."
Zhenbang central region has nearly 27,000 confirmed cases, of which 7,600 are active cases, and there have been 799 deaths related to the coronavirus.
Over the past few weeks, India has seen a surge in confirmed cases, with daily新增cases reaching over 45,000 since July 23.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 is rapidly approaching 1.4 million, with over 467,000 active cases and over 32,000 deaths linked to the virus.
And what could be a more suitable subject for studying rocks on Mars than a rock from Mars itself? So says Professor Caroline Smith, curator of the Meteorite Hall at the Natural History Museum.
Smith is also a member of the science team for the Mars 2020 mission, and he adds that scientists believe the rocks they return came from Mars.
Tiny bubbles within the meteorite are identical to the composition of Mars's atmosphere, which is why we are convinced that this rock came from there.
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was formed when a small asteroid or comet collided with Mars about 60 to 70 million years ago, scattering debris in space.
One rock swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
The meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum since then.
On the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover, there is a high-precision laser instrument named SherLOC, which will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic matter that could indicate the presence or continued existence of life on Mars.
Adding a SAU 008 block is to ensure maximum accuracy for this operation.
Smith added, “We selected this rock particularly because its chemical composition matches ours, and it is a very hard rock.”
One of the Martian meteorites we found is very fragile. But this one is as hard as an old boot.
When the InSight finds rocks of research value, it will store them on the Martian surface.
Then, these rocks will be retrieved by subsequent robotic missions sent to Mars and launched back into Earth for research and analysis.
A man was shot and found nearby Mamboum, near Welybi.
Last night, a man in western Melbourne was shot and rushed to the hospital for emergency treatment.
The police arrived at Mambourin, near Vereby, around 8 PM after receiving an alarm. On Yard Street, they found a 20-year-old man with multiple gunshot wounds to his lower body.
The man was taken to the hospital for treatment and is reportedly in good condition with no life-threatening injuries.
The police said they are investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress up on Sundays; I wear loungewear."
How does your Sunday start?
Tripping over a dog, I stumbled down the stairs on my way to make tea for my wife.
After getting off the ferry, we drove onto A3 Highway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of the work week. Upon arriving home, the kids went to bed. After walking my dog, I had another bowl of cereal.
I would be happy to return from White Island to London, as I know that I could not have a home there if I did not do so.
Kenyan police in Garissa arrested for shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested in connection with an incident that allegedly resulted in two deaths during a shooting.
The shooting occurred on Saturday at the Soko Ng'ombe market in the eastern Garissa County, Kenya, where protesters were rallying against an alleged murder case taking place inside the market.
Two victims are named Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan police watchdog has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The investigation process includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Aden Duale, a Garissa lawmaker, called the incident "heinous" and condemned "police brutality" in a post on Facebook.
Several activists have also expressed concerns about police enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, since March, Kenyan police have caused the deaths of 21 people for non-compliance with curfews and mask-wearing requirements.
At the beginning of this month, police in the capital city of Nairobi released tear gas and arrested several protesters participating in the annual Saba Saba march – which stems from protests 30 years ago opposing former President Daniel arap Moi's dictatorial rule.
A front-line reporter says that protests this year were more radical due to the public anger sparked by the police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdown.
Enhanced security at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security around the American Consulate in Chengdu, China was upgraded, with U.S. personnel preparing to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the building, with uniformed police and plainclothes police lined up along both sides of the street, and metal barriers were set up on the roadside.
China's Global Times editor tweeted that the US Consulate General in Chengdu was ordered to close within 72 hours, which was Monday, July 27 at 10 AM (0200 GMT).
China accuses some personnel at the US Consulate General in Chengdu of engaging in activities inconsistent with their status.
The spokesperson for the Chinese Foreign Ministry, Wang Wenbin, did not provide any details and merely stated that such activities interfere with China's internal affairs and harm China's security interests.
China demanded on Friday that the US consulate in Chengdu be closed as an retaliation to the US demand to close the Chinese consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington demands that China close the Consulate General in Houston “in order to protect American intellectual property and the personal information of Americans.”
Following the closure of the Chinese Consulate-General in Houston on Friday, a group of individuals resembling American officials broke into the facility through a back door.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that the move had seriously violated international law and bilateral agreements, and China would make a response, but did not specify the measures.
Closing embassies in a tit-for-tat manner has further intensified tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage, as well as human rights issues.
The pranksters provided tools to destroy vehicles, and the chimps at the zoo waved around various tools.
Muskhide Wildlife Park in Northside, Mercer County, is notorious for its primates, which attack cars parked within the fence, ripping off wipers or rearview mirrors.
Some witnesses saw baboons waving knives, screwdrivers, even chain saws at the Wildlife World in North Wales, Merseyside.
Staff is worried that pranksters might arm these annoying primates, who could then pounce on stopped cars and rip windshields or mirrors.
Now, staff are concerned that visitors are giving them lethal weapons as "a joke," which is increasing their aggression, and there have even been reports of these baboons rifling through toolboxes.
A staff member of a园区 told The Sunday Times: "We're not sure whether tourists who wanted to see them attack cars supplied the weapons, or whether they stole them from pickup trucks and cargo vans themselves."
"They do indeed rummage through others' toolboxes and carry them around. One has seen baboons dragging a chain saw."
Another worker said, “We have seen baboons carrying small knives and screwdrivers. I was wondering if it was tourists giving them.”
Baboons attack often enough for even the repairmen from northwest England to know about this zoo.
A repairman from Manchester Salford said that people knew what went on at the zoo, and two of his customers were recently attacked by baboons this year.
They said that one minute the children wanted to see the monkeys getting into the cars, and the next minute, the license plates were gone, but such problems have brought good business to repair shops.
Norris Zoo insists it is just as safe as a McDonald’s drive-thru, as long as visitors stay in their cars during a 5-mile wildlife park tour, and reopened on June 15th, implementing safety measures to address the pandemic.
This zoo covers approximately 222.7 hectares and houses animals such as lions, tigers, rhinos, antelopes, and camels, dismissing reports of armed baboons.
A spokesperson said, “We believe that many such stories have been exaggerated through hearsay and embellished, leading to situations where items being found in the park seem unbelievable.”
In 2012, Hyundai, the car manufacturer, placed its new-generation i30 car in the zoo with 40 monkeys for 10 hours to prove the vehicle's strength sufficient to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United and Chelsea enter the UEFA Champions League while Brighton and Hove Albion, Watford drop out of the Premier League.
On Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea qualified for the UEFA Champions League by defeating Leicester City in the final round of Premier League matches, while Brighton & Hove Albion and Watford were relegated, ending their five-year peak struggle.
With Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard scoring, Manchester United beat Leicester City 2-0, securing third place for Ole Gunnar Solskjaer’s team.
Chelsea beat Wolves 2-0 with goals and an assist from Mason Mount, securing fourth place and overtaking Leicester City (which had been in the top four for much of the season) to claim the final spot in the UEFA Champions League.
Leicester City will participate in the European league, and so will Tottenham, which drew 1-1 with Wolves in a match at Crystal Palace and ultimately advanced due to a better goal difference than Wolves.
With the play-off for the remaining two relegation spots coming down to the final moments, Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute, only to be equalized one minute later by West Ham, leaving them level at 1-1.
Captain Jack Grealish scored a goal for Aston Villa, which could be his last match playing for his boyhood club.
Bournemouth defeated Everton 3-1 but finished one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford were relegated as the second-from-last team after losing 3–2 to Arsenal.
Manchester United scored over 100 goals during the league season and won 5-0 against relegated Norwich, earning their spot in the Europa League. This game was also David Silva's final match in the Premier League, where he had been playing for 10 years.
Kevin De Bruyne sets a record by scoring twice and assisting 20 times this season.
Liverpool beat Newcastle United 3-1 to end their title-winning campaign of the season, finishing with a total of 99 points.
Additionally, Southampton defeated Sheffield United 3-1 this season, with Danny Ings scoring 22 goals this season.
And Jamie Vardy, a player from Leicester City, scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes to instill good "values" in her children
Gwyneth Paltrow says she tries to educate her children not just "by giving them fish," but also by helping them develop good values.
Paltrow has two children with ex-husband Chris Martin, ages 16 (Apple) and 14 (Moses), and she hopes they will uphold good values rather than rely on their parents' fame.
"That's why I believe... instilling a child's ethics and values so that they align with either yours or their own self-identity is important," she told Rob Lowe in the podcast.
What actually happens is that I can show you the way, but if you just try like everyone else, you won't get anywhere.
She adds: "As parents, what I can do for my children is teach them right from wrong and what work is like. Indeed, I tried to avoid just teaching them how to fish."
Paltrow also took steps to keep children’s lives as private as possible, ensuring that their social media accounts remained private.
Considering there was still an upcoming election, the Renewal Party eventually agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz says Pelosi's aim is "to solve problems with money and force a government shutdown"
Washington –– Republican Senator Ted Cruz of Texas accused House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Democratic majority in the House of pushing for the coronavirus relief package, which he said was aimed at “spending money to fix problems and shut down America.” Negotiations on subsequent measures are ongoing.
Cruz said on Sunday during an interview with "Face the Nation," that the United States is facing two crises simultaneously: the pandemic that has taken more than 600,000 lives worldwide, and an "absolute economic catastrophe."
Cruz criticized Pelosi, who had been interviewed by "For the People," for “not addressing any of these issues.”
Cruz said that her goal was to use money to solve the problem and prevent a government shutdown in the US.
You should especially look at the $3 trillion bill she is trying to push, which is nothing more than spending money without actually solving the problem. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work so they can support their families.
Senate Republicans and the White House last week negotiated the terms of the fourth phase plan to address the pandemic, which is expected to total $1 trillion.
Last weekend, Chief of Staff Mark Meadows and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Capitol Hill to further discuss with Senate staff. On Sunday, Mnuchin, in an interview with Fox News Channel, stated that Majority Leader Mitch McConnell of the Senate would unveil the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue facing lawmakers and the focus of the debate between Republicans and Democrats is that extended unemployment benefits have expired and will be canceled this weekend.
The Democrats want the “Phase IV” coronavirus relief package to maintain the $600 in additional aid, while Republicans called for cutting that amount and arguing that some Americans receive unemployment benefits that exceed their wages.
Cruz notes that half or more Americans receiving enhanced unemployment benefits now receive more than they earned working. He says he learned from talking to small business owners in Texas that some are having trouble hiring due to this.
He says, “Of course they won’t come back to work, because the federal government is paying them sometimes twice as much as their wages.”
Cruz believes that Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill that lowers taxes for small businesses and relaxes regulations so that Americans can return to work.
He says, “The recovery bill is not about spending trillions of dollars, but pausing payroll taxes, which effectively gives a raise to every working American. This way truly gets people back to work.”
Trump asked for the next rescue package to include a payroll tax cut last week, but Mnuchin said the proposal would not go through the legislative process because the Republicans do not accept it.
President Trump confirms that the next bill does not include a payroll tax cut, but tries to shift the blame to the Democrats.
Last week, Cruz objected to the cost of the new proposal, stating on Sunday that he disagreed with the measure proposed by Senate Republicans.
But he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed by the Democratic-controlled House in May, accusing his opponents of deliberately crafting measures that would harm the President’s interests before the November election.
He said, “The Democrats’ only goal is to defeat Donald Trump, and they naively believe that shutting down every aspect of America and closing every school in the country is the best way to do so.”
Prime Minister Narendra Modi mourns the Kargil heroes and says Pakistan tried to stab India in the back.
Today, it is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, and Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi, while paying his respects to the martyred armed forces, stated that despite India’s efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan continues to try to stab India in the back.
Prime Minister Modi said during his monthly radio program "Mann Ki Baat," "Pakistan caused this disaster; they have devised cunning plans to seize Indian land and divert its internal conflicts."
He said, “Pakistan is shameless for trying to occupy Indian territory and diverting public attention from its internal turmoil.”
At the time, India was trying to mend its relations with Pakistan.
But, as is the case with evil, its essence is to fight against everyone for no reason.
Such people will only do evil, even if the other party wants to do them a good deed."
He explained, “That is why Pakistan went ahead and stabbed India in the back despite efforts by New Delhi to maintain the relationship.”
He added, “Imagine the enemy entrenched high on the hilltops, while our troops fight them below. However, our high spirits and true strength helped us defeat the enemy atop the mountains.”
"Our courageous armed forces demonstrated India's great power in the Kargil War," Prime Minister Modi said, recalling what he had seen and experienced during his visit to the war zone.
India celebrates an event annually to commemorate the Kargil victory, marking the day India's army recaptured all Indian outposts in Ladakh that were occupied by Pakistani forces nearly 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced the successful conclusion of the "Vijay Operation."
The war fought on snowy heights in the Ladakh region lasted nearly three months and resulted in the death of over 500 Indian soldiers.
Earlier today, several senior officials and military leaders also paid their respects at the "Unknown Warrior Memorial" (Amar Jawan Jyoti) at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of Staff Bipin Rawat, Army Chief MM Naravane, Air Force Chief R K S Bhadauria, and Navy Admiral Karambir Singh.
For decades after the Kargil War, India and Pakistan’s relations have been strained by a series of terrorist attacks.
India insists that negotiations and terrorist attacks cannot occur simultaneously, and Pakistan needs to dismantle all terrorist camps operating on its territory.
Online lending firms issue warning: SME loan schemes may not achieve their goals.
Small enterprises are often overlooked by large banks, and thus they increasingly turn to online lending institutions to meet their funding needs.
However, Mr. Callaghan noted that peer-to-peer lenders do not have an advantage over large banks, as they cannot access the same cheap funding, and he also warned that the small and medium-sized enterprise guarantee scheme is unpopular.
He said, “I believe this proposal is likely to fail because people call us expecting very cheap funds, rather than half of the loan amount coming from the government.”
So, after we called them, they were disappointed because someone had requested a discount price, but our financing costs did not change at all.
Mr. Callaghan added that such a scheme would be “a piece of cake” if fintech enterprises were able to obtain cheaper financing and then transfer the funds to small businesses.
This is somewhat of a dilemma – large banks can obtain funds at lower costs, but they prefer to avoid risks, so they do not lend in the same way. They will not access data unless there is collateral, in which case they feel secure. Otherwise, they would be uncomfortable, ” he said. “Unless we can get cheaper funding, I don’t think the market has any illusions about the popularity of this solution.”
Moula in Melbourne also participated in the formulation of the plan, CEO Aris Allegos stated that the 50% guarantee yield suggested in the government scheme should be transferred to small businesses, but different lending institutions would handle it differently.
He says, "I can understand that others might not have experienced such a cost structure."
This could involve their funding structure, as well as whom they lend to and how they assess the risk of those categories.
Dr. Allegos declined to disclose how much financing Moulah had disbursed under the initial proposal, but stated that the revised plan “significantly reflects what needs to be changed,” thereby offering greater flexibility to both borrowers and lenders.
However, he is very concerned about the “two-speed economy” that is running and its impact on small businesses.
He says that for small businesses, the biggest challenges that Mula and lenders face are often referred to as the “cliff” and how to manage it.
Overall, while there has been some growth in demand, it has not yet returned to pre-pandemic levels.
网贷机构 Prospa, listed on the Australian Securities Exchange, also declined to disclose the amount of loans it has disbursed under the scheme, citing such information as commercially sensitive, but CEO Greg Moshal noted that the government’s expansion of the scheme has been well-received.
He said, “We support various enhancements to the solution that enable small businesses to access a range of financing products that meet their needs, including flexible short-term cashflow products.”
Virginia Beach removes monument to the Confederacy
Virginia Beach (Virginia) — On Sunday, Virginia Beach City Council removed a 115-year-old Confederate Memorial from the public park. Two days prior, the city council had unanimously voted to remove it.
According to media reports, workers dismantled the 27-foot (8.2-meter) tall monument on Saturday morning.
The monument stood outside the former Princess Anne County Courthouse in 1905, where slave auctions也曾 took place.
The City Council unanimously decided to proceed with the demolition work that night on Thursday.
The city council heard from dozens of speakers who view the monument as division for many and as an ever-present reminder of painful pasts.
Given recent protests and vandalism against other monuments, the monument in question has been completely covered and surrounded by locked gates.
The monument will be temporarily retained.
The city now needs to wait for a response from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to handle this monument.
Protests continue as police attempt to use new less-lethal tools.
In Portland and across the country, violent clashes continue between law enforcement and protesters, with debates raging about how to reduce police use of lethal force.
Some companies are ramping up efforts to provide new high-tech tools like police remote handcuffs, as alternatives to more dangerous weapons such as pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flash bangs.
Amnesty International analyzed 500 videos from protests from May 26 to June 5 and found instances of excessive use of force in 125 cases across 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Brian Castner, an Arms Investigation Officer from International Crisis Group, said, "We saw cases of disproportionate use of tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40mm impact rounds, which are used in inappropriate combinations of less-lethal weapons."
Some still hold the view that choice is important for avoiding the use of guns.
Corey Pegues, a former deputy inspector of the New York Police Department, stated that “we should equip officers with various non-lethal personal protective gear rather than lethal weapons such as guns, because policing is the only profession in America where citizens grant power to take a life.”
There is a new tool called the Bola Wrap, a handheld device that can shoot a 8-foot Kevlar rope to ensnare suspects.
Other new types of equipment include long-range acoustic devices (also known as acoustic cannons), directed energy weapons that use lasers to heat human skin, new artillery shells made from materials like foam and chalk powder, teargas, and tasers.
Less than a week after the death of George Floyd at the hands of police, protests turned violent, and the stock price of gun maker Axon surged over 18%, hitting an all-time high.
It is预计 that the market for non-lethal weapons will reach $11.85 billion by 2023.
Watch the video to learn about the new high-tech non-lethal tools used by police, how to regulate their use, and what risks these tools may pose to future policing and communities.
The UN says that Afghanistan is hosting thousands of Pakistani Taliban militants.
Islamabad –- A UN report said that more than 6,000 Pakistani militants are hiding in Afghanistan, with the majority of them belonging to illegal Pakistani Taliban groups responsible for attacking military and civilian targets in Pakistan.
According to a report released this week, the organization known as the Tehrik-i-Taliban (TTP) has links with the Islamic State's (ISIS) branch in Afghanistan.
Some members of TTP even joined an affiliate of IS located in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to an Associated Press request for comment on related issues.
Reports indicate that the IS (IS of Helmand province) in Afghanistan has been severely hit by Afghan security forces and US and NATO forces, and sometimes even targeted by the Taliban.
The report was written by a team supporting analysis and sanctions monitoring, which tracks terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that ISIS’s ranks in Afghanistan number around 2,200 members, although its leadership has been weakened, the group still considers a Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, one of its leaders.
The report also notes that monitoring teams reported that two senior ISIS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, had arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report notes that although Islamic State (IS)-held territory is shrinking, they still have the capability to carry out attacks of some scale in various regions of the country, including Kabul.
The group also wants to win over Taliban fighters who oppose signing an agreement with the United States, referring to the peace deal signed between the US and the Taliban in February.
The objective of this agreement is to allow the US to end its 19-year war in Afghanistan, and to hope that the Taliban will guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
The agreement is also expected to ensure that the Taliban will fully commit to fighting against IS.
The second part of the agreement – and perhaps the most important part – calls for talks between Taliban leaders and political representatives in Kabul.
Later that Saturday, the U.S. State Department issued a statement saying that its peace envoy Zalmay Khalilzad was still busy in the region, trying to push stalled negotiations that have been repeatedly delayed due to disagreements over prisoner-release plans.
This US-backed agreement calls for the Afghan government to release 5,000 Taliban prisoners in exchange for Taliban releases of 1,000 government and military personnel as a goodwill gesture prior to negotiations.
Currently, the Afghan government is refusing to release approximately 600 Taliban prisoners, labeling them as high-profile criminals, but is proposing to release the remaining prisoners.
The Taliban refused the government's proposal.
"This is an opportunity for both sides to come closest yet to engaging in intra-Afghan negotiations and a critical step toward ending the 40-year-long Afghan war," the U.S. State Department said.
"While important progress has been made in prisoner exchanges, further efforts are needed to solve the problem."
Earlier this week, the Taliban’s political spokesman said that the Taliban plan to hold talks with Kabul’s political leaders after Eid al-Adha (an Islamic holiday) next month, provided that the prisoner release work is completed first.
The biggest concern for Pakistan is the militants within Afghanistan, particularly those linked to the TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, and Lashkar-e-Islam, or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched a large-scale attack in southern Sindh Province and southwestern Balochistan in that month.
Multiple Pakistani military personnel were killed in clashes with militants in southwestern Balochistan province.
TTP organization conducted a highly terroristic attack on Pakistan in 2014, resulting in the death of 140 members of the Pakistani Army College.
Most of them are students, some as young as 5 years old.
The report states that "Afghanistan-based Pakistani foreign terrorist operatives pose a threat to the security of both countries, with an estimated number ranging from 6,000 to 6,500 people, most of whom are members of the TTP."
HSBC denies setting up "traps" for Chinese company Huawei in an investigation in the US – RT Business News
HSBC, headquartered in London, through social media stated that the bank did not fabricate evidence as reported by Chinese media to have led to the arrest of Huawei's chief financial officer in Canada.
A WeChat article that is no longer available states that banks claimed their actions did not prompt an investigation by the US into Huawei, the world’s largest supplier of telecommunications network equipment, as scrutiny of Huawei’s alleged violations of US sanctions against Iran had already begun well before the banks’ involvement, in late 2016.
HSBC stated on Saturday that it "has no hostile intent towards Huawei and there is nothing 'frame' Huawei," and added that it would never distort any facts about its clients.
In response to the information requested by the U.S. Department of Justice (US DOJ), HSBC Group merely stated objective facts.
HSBC did not fabricate the evidence nor conceal the facts.”
The platform blocked this article and flagged it as "violation," making the content currently unavailable.
After multiple Chinese media outlets, including state-backed newspapers, accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up traps for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this clarification.
In December 2018, Canadian authorities arrested Huawei's CFO Meng Wanzhou at the request of US agencies, and for nearly a year and a half, the company has been a focal point in diplomatic news.
Ma Xiaohu was charged with fraud for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and failing to disclose the relationship between Huawei and Iranian company Skycom to banks.
The new evidence cited by The Global Times shows that prior to this, HSBC was well aware of the relationship between the Chinese company and Skycom.
It appears that the bank chose to “collude” with the US government in order to avoid harsher penalties imposed in 2012, when HSBC had already been forced to admit to money laundering and violations of sanctions, including the ban on Iran.
At that time, the bank was ordered to pay around $1.9 billion in fines and needed to implement measures to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article on the matter, the newspaper wrote: “There is evidence that HSBC knew about this relationship clearly but deliberately sold its customers’ confidential business documents to美方 in order to have the US government overlook the British bank’s misconduct and evade the sanctions it should have faced under the Deferred Prosecution Agreement of 2012.”
This state-controlled Chinese newspaper adds in an open warning that, should any collusion between banks be proven, such entities may face "severe punishment" in China (one of HSBC’s key markets).
In recent weeks, this well-known bank has been the target of repeated criticism.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting the new national security law implemented in Hong Kong, where the bank originated and contributes half of its profits.
HSBC finds itself in an unenviable position: in the West, US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “bowing and scraping”; in the East, Chinese media hail its “late but welcome support”.
Angola: Pedalé Hospital Handover to Ministry of Health Oversight
Rwanda – Republic President João Lourenço has transferred the supervision of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital under construction in Rwanda to the Ministry of Health this Friday.
The Office of the President of the Republic stated in a press release that this move is aimed at implementing the government's construction principles, expanding the network of hospitals, and ensuring better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also notes that "in this current exceptional situation, this measure allows the Angolan people to have a distinctive, valuable reference hospital, equipped with modern technology and precision instruments for diagnosing and treating complex diseases."
The decision of Angola’s president also takes into account the country's population growth, which has fueled a "huge demand" for the most diverse range of high-quality healthcare services.
The document also notes this demand as reasonable, since hospitals encompass complex disease treatment, teaching, research, and interventions related to ophthalmology and neonatal medicine, areas whose strategies and management should fall under the purview of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama – Algeria has lost a patriot and a steadfast warrior.
Alger – President of the Republic Abdelmadjid Tebboune expressed his condolences for Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, a deceased member of the National People’s Assembly who passed away on Friday, describing Algeria as “a nation that has lost a patriot and a warrior dedicated to the cause of the people and the country.”
President Tebboune wrote in his condolences: "Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has left us. Algeria has lost a patriot, a warrior who devoted himself to the nation and people's cause, whose determination never wavered from the moment he picked up arms, fighting alongside the valiant mujahedeen brothers until his last breath, which we can only listen to God’s decree on this day of our grief."
"Late Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace, came from a special generation of believers who sacrificed themselves for their country as patriotic fighters."
"In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp of Wilaya VI at the height of their lives, joining the ranks of the revolution and freeing Algeria from the claws of inferior colonialism, sacrificing their lives."
The late leader was battle-hardened in building a united and prosperous Algeria, and his name became known to all.
The President added: “In the far south, Mahmoud Ghemmama is revered as a mediator. He steps in once discord is kindled, helping his fellow citizens to reconcile.”
These qualities and values won Mahmoud Guemama the respect and trust of the people of Tamanrasset, electing him as a representative in the National People's Assembly for multiple terms, a messenger and defender of the region's concerns and aspirations of the people, and the voice of all Algerian people.
No eulogy could do justice to the tribute of Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace.
"Today, he left us at the most critical moment when Algeria needs him," said the President.
Finally, President Tebboune said, “I pray to the Almighty God that the deceased may be bathed in the sanctity and mercy of your light, welcoming his arrival in the boundless heaven alongside the shuhada and the pious.”
I beg the Lord to give his family and loved ones patience and comfort…only you have such supreme power.”
Travel Guide for Coronavirus Pandemic: Countries Implementing Entry Restrictions for Bulgarian Passengers
More countries are imposing travel restrictions. Starting this week, the Czech Republic wants to conduct coronavirus tests on seasonal workers from Bulgaria. Denmark has mandated that only Bulgarian citizens who have registered residency, have employment contracts, or hold proof of business travel can enter the country.
Starting today, people who have visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must undergo mandatory quarantine in Italy. Finland will only accept travelers with 'valid reasons' for entry.
Austria requires mandatory quarantine at home even if PCR test results are negative. Germany also implements the same policy for arrivals. For the next 14 days, entry is not permitted to southern Romania without sufficient reason. Greece has taken stricter measures on its land borders.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that from Tuesday on, passengers flying to Greece from Bulgaria must present a negative PCR test for新冠病毒 upon arrival at Greek border authorities, with the test having been completed within 72 hours prior to their entry into Greece's southern neighboring country.
They also announced that travelers must present corresponding test certificates to be allowed entry. The certificates must be issued in English and include the three names and number found on the traveler's passport or ID. If the certificates do not meet these requirements, authorities in neighboring southern countries will not accept them.
Passengers need to have RT-PCR tests conducted at the laboratory by collecting nasopharyngeal or oropharyngeal secretions through extraction. Accepted test results include those obtained from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs that have been accredited by relevant national certification bodies (not necessarily specifically for COVID-19), after undergoing a test for COVID-19.
Greek citizens, residents in Greece, and travelers with significant professional reasons are exempt from this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that passengers arriving in Greece on Tuesday, July 28, and Wednesday, July 29, who did not get tested before their departure will undergo testing upon arrival by Greek authorities.
The following requirements still apply: passengers traveling to Greece by plane must fill out the electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) and generate a QR code before departing at least 24 hours in advance, and submit this form to Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information, see travel.gov.gr.
Currently, there have been no changes to requirements for land border crossings between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explained that "all passengers entering Greece via the land border, regardless of their nationality, need to fill out an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) and generate a QR code at least 24 hours before arrival."
The closure of the Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda, and Makaza checkpoints has increased traffic flow near the border of Petrich. Crossing is not an issue, and there are no queues of trucks. Everyone is waiting to go sea from Greece. Passengers need to wait about an hour and a half to cross.
There are three checkpoints open on the Bulgarian-Greek border. Currently, "Ilinden - Exohi" and the segment in Ivaylovgrad are closed.
Dominic Cummings visits the SAS base and is held as a hostage.
The Prime Minister's chief advisor experienced the feeling of being held hostage last week during a visit to the facility.
For some harassed civil servants, the idea of holding Dominic Cummings hostage by pointing a gun at his head might have a certain allure.
The强硬 Prime Minister’s chief adviser experienced first-hand what it was like when he visited SAS headquarters at Stirling Castle last week.
The Sunday Mail reported that part of his itinerary on Wednesday included visiting an "indoors counter-terrorism training room," where elite soldiers hone their skills in dealing with terrorists.
He also toured the Hereford base under guidance, inspected the military equipment of the brigade, heard a briefing on recent operations, including efforts to clear Islamic State terrorists from Iraq.
The important visitors to the Stansted防线 were given a special welcome – experiencing the "Close Quarters Combat Pod" inside the two-story building where SAS trains close-quarters fighting.
Princess Diana had visited here as well; in a training exercise in 1983, her hair had accidentally caught fire from the sparks of a thrown grenade.
It is reported that Mr. Cummings arrived by helicopter, and the base is named after Colonel David Stirling, founder of the regiment.
He also visited the nearby Pontrilas barracks where other secretive units were stationed.
This visit is one stop on his tour of highly classified British security facilities, which has garnered attention from politicians and military leaders.
When news of its plan leaked earlier this month, Conservative Backbencher and Chair of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee Tobias Ellwood said that Members of Parliament should be afforded the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to question defence and security matters.
Rarely does an inquiry require a special advisor appointed by politicians to provide testimony.
According to reports, SAS senior officials used the activities on Wednesday to highlight the importance of maintaining the numbers of the Royal Marines and the Parachute Regiment.
As previously reported, units that have been sending large numbers of personnel to special forces might be downsized or merged to save costs.
A defense source said: "SAS consists of 250 soldiers, half of whom were previously paratroopers. The size of the Special Boat Squadron (SBS) is smaller; without the support of the Royal Marines, it may be difficult to sustain."
"Cummings was told that these units must be fully retained."
Mr. Cummings had previously complained that "defense procurement continues to waste billions of pounds," and he recently attended a meeting held at the headquarters of MI5 and MI6, where there were plans to visit the SBS headquarters in Poole, Dorset, and the National Cryptological Laboratory in Broughton, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of Defense did not respond to a request for comment last night.
"Sunday Morning" Dayshow: Honoring Rodgers and Hart
2020 was the centenary of Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart's iconic partnership, one of Broadway's most legendary musical theater duos.
In honor of these two songwriters, Broadway star Jelani Alladin (from Frozen and Hercules), Christiani Pitts (from Kung Fu Panda and Moana), and dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (from The Book of Mormon and Shuffle Along) performed a modern take on "Lady Down Below," a rendition based on Rodgers and Hart’s original work, specially recorded for the audience of Sunday in the Park.
Bassist Russell Hall performs alongside drummer Bryan Carter, who provides the musical direction and arrangement for the show.
California’s Lassen County wildfire is under control.
Two wildfires have broken out in Lassen County, located northeast of Sacramento County in California, with an area burned exceeding 12,141 hectares. Firefighters are still battling to contain the fires.
The California fire department said that as of Saturday morning, the "Gold" wildfire burning in the southern part of the Addy region was only 40% contained, with large numbers of homes and ranches still at risk from the blaze, which could have a major impact on local agriculture, livestock, and forestry sectors.
The fire has been burning for four days, having destroyed approximately 8,498 hectares of land and seven houses, and injuring two firefighters who were taken to hospital.
Firefighters worked overnight to breach and reinforce the fireline under limited conditions.
But according to the weather forecast, it was expected to be windy and low humidity on that day.
The "Hog" wildfire that has burned in Susanville West has destroyed two buildings and about 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection stated that after firefighters worked all night to clear and reinforce the fireline, the fire is now under control.
As of Saturday morning, the wildfire had been burning for six days, with 45% of it brought under control.
Be careful of those seemingly attractive gardening schemes.
It may be an embarrassing matter for botanists, but I am indeed very much addicted to gardening tricks.
Positively speaking, I tend to be an open-minded person, or at least somewhat irrational optimist, and many things in the plant world are often beyond intuitive judgment.
But I do not shy away from admitting that I have fallen into this gardening trick numerous times.
While I firmly believe in the value of trying new things, I've managed to avoid some of the most common traps in gardening that I've encountered by not going for the "hellish" solutions.
First, perhaps the biggest red flag – grafting tomatoes onto potatoes.
Transplanting tomatoes onto potatoes was hailed as a revolutionary breakthrough, though in fact it is an old trick that was attempted back in the 1920s.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family, with their internal “pipes” being fairly similar, and in fact, grafting can create miracle enhancements.
But the problem is that creating resource-intensive structures like fruits and root crops requires a large amount of energy for metabolism, and grafted plants need to satisfy the energy needs of both crops simultaneously, which limits the allocation of limited energy.
This means that the yields of these grafted plants are very low, quite different from what you see in the promotional pictures.
Moreover, the time of crop maturity varies, and potatoes need to be harvested before tomatoes that have not yet ripened.
Similarly, the promotional images also contain many misleading gimmicks.
I have seen roses processed to a sky blue color using Photoshop in some directories, and the number of fruits on some trees is simply unbelievable.
Some merchants even “borrow” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim them to be part of their test bases on online shopping websites.
These unscrupulous merchants' practices make you have to view the promotional images, especially those from the internet, with a skeptical eye.
If it looks too good to be true, it probably is.
Talking about blue roses, blue is relatively rare in the plant kingdom, so if you see new varieties of shining holly bushes, orchids, or cacti under high concentrations of what appears to be "disinfectant," it's likely that they have been sprayed or dyed.
If the vendor explicitly states the situation, there is nothing wrong with that, but they rarely do so.
The blue butterfly orchid will revert to its original white, but cacti and holly ivy will quickly wilt after being coated with an anti-reflective dye.
Finally, I must reveal the bonsai set that promises to grow miniature trees, and there are even worse bonsai seeds than these sets.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Kiosks are typically mature shrubs that are then shaped and guide new growth directions with wires.
I am clear that I will continue to be enamored by these floral tricks, which is one of the joys of experimentation.
But avoiding these issues, you may have the chance to purchase an item that matches what the seller displays.
Do you need to quarantine in Spain?
No, but if you return to Spain after 12:00 PM tonight, you must self-quarantine at home for 14 days.
UK Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said: "We decided earlier this week not to enforce quarantine measures for those returning from Spain because data shows an improvement in the spread of the virus."
But clearly, the latest data warrants our concern, which forces us to overturn the original decision.
We know it might be disappointing, but we have always clearly indicated that we will closely monitor the situation in different countries. If the virus starts to flare up again in any country, we might ask for its name to be removed from the list of those not implementing isolation measures.”
Viruses remain rampant and deadly. Our top priority is to contain the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health.
The government has not yet released regulations for UK nationals visiting Spain, but you should check the rules in your area if you are planning to visit Spain.
Spanish Deputy Chief of Emergency Health, Maria Jose Sierra said:
"There has been a major outbreak here. This may be the second wave. We need to keep an eye on the situation over the next few weeks."
Imam-Ul-Haq of the Pakistan team continued to compete despite sustaining an injury during warm-up.
The Pakistan team and the England team will face each other in three Test matches and three T20Is.
Pakistan cricket player Imam-ul-Haq suffered an injury to his left hand during a intra-team warm-up match for the derby, and the team doctor declared him fit to play.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a release: "The left-handed cricketer took part in a warm-up match before the game on the second day."
On the first day of the tournament, when PCB White Team's Imam and PCB Green Team's Naseem Shah exchanged punches, Imam was hit in the left hand.
On Friday evening, Imam suffered an injury to his left hand after being hit by a ball thrown by Shah and was forced to withdraw from the game, but he participated in the morning warm-up match and will return to the field on the third day.
Over 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government departments and a state-owned bank in Tiruvarur, Tamil Nadu, India, said that at least 38 employees working in the main branch of the bank in Tiruvarur tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials say they have advised customers who visited the branch to voluntarily participate in virus testing.
Previously, a senior employee of the branch had other complications and later died from COVID-19.
Officials said that recently, they conducted a broad medical screening at the branch, which led to the discovery of these positive cases.
Bank officials say they have completed the disinfection and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are contacting customers who visited that bank.
Tamil Nadu is India’s second-most affected state in terms of确诊病例 numbers, with the Maharashtra reporting the highest number of infections. As of Saturday, the total number of confirmed cases reported by Tamil Nadu had reached 206,737.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built on a disputed site.
New Delhi (AP) – A trustee overseeing temple construction said Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi plans to attend next month the groundbreaking ceremony for a Hindu temple being built on a disputed site in northern India, where a mosque that dated back to the 16th century was destroyed by Hindu extremists in 1992.
The ceremony is set to take place on August 5th, when organizers refer to it as a sacred day for Hindus, though it also marks one year since India's parliament revoked the special autonomous status of Jammu and Kashmir, which was then a Muslim-majority state.
Modi’s supporters and opponents in India’s Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) will not miss this symbolic day, when the party has long promised to take back Kashmir’s autonomy and build a temple dedicated to the Hindu deity Ram on the site of a mosque that predates the Mughal era, with believers claiming it is the birthplace of Ram. The site is located in Ayodhya, Uttar Pradesh.
The coronavirus continues to spread across India, where it is reported the country has become the third-most infected in the world. In light of this, the Hindu nationalist organization Vishwa Hindu Parishad, which has allies in the ruling Bharatiya Janata Party, has announced that the ceremony will be broadcast live on national TV and that attendance will be limited both to participants and viewers.
The organization's spokesperson, Vinod Bansal, said at a press conference on Saturday that the temple will become "a perpetual center for social harmony, national unity and integration, awakening of Hinduism."
Following the overwhelming victory of the Bharatiya Janata Party in the election, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the temple trust, stating that five acres should be diverted elsewhere for use by Muslim petitioners.
Hindu extremists have long claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the existing temples in the ancient city of Ayodhya.
Violent community incidents that followed the demolition of the mosque in December 1992 led to numerous killings; an estimated 2,000 people were killed, with the majority being Muslim.
Meanwhile, the special court is still hearing the case of the mosque demolition.
An architect from Ahmedabad, Gujarat, India, hometown of Modi, proposes building a tower-shaped sandstone structure temple that is 161 feet (49 meters) high with five domes.
